# Royal Elementor Addons Widget Guidance

When creating or changing REA widgets, inspect nearby free/pro widgets first and keep the implementation aligned with the current module system unless a cleaner approach clearly improves maintainability.

## Structure and Registration

- Free widgets live in `royal-elementor-addons/modules/<slug>/widgets/`; Pro widgets live in `royal-elementor-addons-pro/modules/<slug>-pro/widgets/` or the matching theme-builder path.
- Each widget module normally has `module.php` with namespace `WprAddons\Modules\<StudlySlug>` or `WprAddonsPro\Modules\<StudlySlug>`, extends the matching `Module_Base`, returns the widget class name from `get_widgets()`, and returns the widget slug from `get_name()`.
- Module managers derive namespaces from slugs by converting kebab-case to StudlyCase, so folder slug, module namespace, widget namespace, class name, and `get_name()` must stay in sync.
- Register new free widgets in `WprAddons\Classes\Utilities::get_registered_modules()` or the relevant theme/Woo module list. Register Pro widgets in `WprAddonsPro\Classes\Pro_Modules` and add the free-side premium/admin toggle or promotion entry when the widget should appear before Pro is active.
- Use Elementor categories already registered by REA: `wpr-widgets`, `wpr-premium-widgets`, `wpr-theme-builder-widgets`, or `wpr-woocommerce-builder-widgets`.

## Widget Class Conventions

- Widgets extend `Elementor\Widget_Base`; class names usually use `Wpr_Foo_Bar` or `Wpr_Foo_Bar_Pro`, with slugs like `wpr-foo-bar` or `wpr-foo-bar-pro`.
- Provide `get_name()`, `get_title()`, `get_icon()`, `get_categories()`, and useful `get_keywords()`. Use text domain `wpr-addons` for both free and pro.
- Prefer `esc_html__()` for plain labels, `esc_html_e()` for direct output, `esc_attr()` for attributes, `esc_url()` for URLs, and `wp_kses_post()` or narrow `wp_kses()` only when HTML is intentionally allowed.
- For Elementor optimized markup support, follow nearby widgets that implement `has_widget_inner_wrapper()` and adjust selectors when wrapper markup changes.
- Keep controls grouped by Elementor tabs/sections, use `selectors`/CSS variables for styling controls, and prefer REA helpers such as `Utilities::wpr_library_buttons()`, `upgrade_pro_notice()`, and `pro_features_list_section()` where the existing widget family uses them.
- **Library buttons:** Call `Utilities::wpr_library_buttons( $this, Controls_Manager::RAW_HTML );` at the top of the first content section when the widget should show Widget Preview / Predefined Styles in the panel. The helper outputs a wrapper `div` with class equal to `get_name()` (e.g. `wpr-unfold`). If the widget has no predefined styles library (preview only), hide the second link in `assets/css/admin/editor.min.css` by adding `.elementor-control-wpr_library_buttons .<widget-slug> a:last-child` to the existing hide rule block (same list as `wpr-forms`, `wpr-phone-call`, etc.).

## Assets, Output, and Quality

- Runtime frontend CSS belongs in `assets/css/frontend.min.css` only (`frontend.css` is ignored—see `frontend-css-min-only.mdc`). Runtime frontend JS belongs in `assets/js/frontend.min.js` only. Do not edit the unminified bundles.
- For layout-dependent widgets (sliders, grids, carousels, masonry, async API output), hide the main wrapper with `opacity: 0` in `frontend.min.css` until frontend JS finishes layout/init, then set `opacity: 1` (see `.wpr-grid`, `.wpr-advanced-slider`, `.wpr-google-reviews-wrap`). Use a short `transition: opacity 0.3s ease` when fading in. In Elementor editor, keep the widget visible with `.elementor-editor-active .elementor-widget-<slug> .<wrapper>` so the panel preview is not blank before JS runs. Always reveal on init failure too (`Promise.all(...).catch(reveal)` or an equivalent fallback) so errors/messages are not permanently hidden.
- Add `get_script_depends()` / `get_style_depends()` only when a widget needs registered dependencies; reuse handles registered in `plugin.php` before adding new assets.
- In `render()`, use `$this->get_settings_for_display()`, escape every dynamic output, sanitize request/API inputs, cache remote API responses with transients where appropriate, and render editor-friendly demo/error states when external data is missing.
- Avoid inline styles/scripts in PHP render methods except tiny values that must be dynamic and cannot be expressed through Elementor selectors or CSS variables.
- Use existing patterns as the baseline, but recommend or implement better abstractions when they reduce real duplication, clarify free/pro behavior, or make a new widget safer without widening the task unnecessarily.
